#   method   each row is a CHILD process with grpc's own tracers on; the answers are
#            read out of grpc's trace, not out of a document

grpc++ 1.51.1, protobuf C++ 3021012. Each row is a CHILD process with
GRPC_TRACE=http,flowctl,bdp_estimator and GRPC_VERBOSITY=DEBUG, running 8x8
P2.2 Fetches over loopback TCP against a server left at its DEFAULTS, so the
only variable is the client's configuration.

client configuration         announced last announced  settings  BDPping   stalls  min t_win  min s_win
grpcpp default                 4194303       6799830         3        3        1          0    3782954
grpcpp win 4M                  4194303       6945113         3        3        1          0    3782954
grpcpp bdp off                   65535         65535         1        0      108          0          0
grpcpp win 4M bdp off          4194304       4194304         1        0      259          0    3653887
grpcpp win 64M                 4194303       5127941         2        3        1          0    3782954
grpcpp win 64M bdp off        67108864      67108864         1        0      232          0   66568447
core default                   2097152       2097152         1        0        0         -1         -1
core win 4M conn 4M            4194304       4194304         1        0        7          0    3782989
core win 4M conn 64K           4194304       4194304         1        0     4823          0    3655072

`announced` is SETTINGS_INITIAL_WINDOW_SIZE as the SERVER received it, so it
is what the client asked its peer to respect -- the window the 540 KB response
has to fit inside. `settings` counts how many times the client re-announced it,
which is auto-tuning made visible. `BDPping` counts the CLIENT transport's
pings. `stalls` counts writing.cc's 'moved to stalled list' with the two
windows printed, and `min t_win` / `min s_win` are the CONNECTION and STREAM
windows at their lowest across those stalls. A stall at t_win=0 with s_win
large is the connection window binding while the stream window is idle.

-- SHAPES.md's two traps, answered from the rows above rather than inherited --

1. ARE THE STREAM AND CONNECTION WINDOWS SEPARATE CHANNEL ARGUMENTS?
   Two different answers for the two stacks, and neither is grpc-java's.

   grpc++: they are separate QUANTITIES and only one of them is reachable.
   grpc/impl/codegen/grpc_types.h exposes GRPC_ARG_HTTP2_STREAM_LOOKAHEAD_BYTES
   and GRPC_ARG_HTTP2_BDP_PROBE and NO connection-window argument at all. That
   they are separate is observed and not argued: `grpcpp win 4M bdp off` stalls
   259 times at t_win=0 with s_win=3653887, and raising the stream window sixteen
   times over to 64 MiB (`grpcpp win 64M bdp off`) leaves 232 stalls at
   t_win=0 -- the argument does not reach the connection window, so it cannot
   be pinned from a channel argument.

   The core (tonic 0.14 / hyper 1.11): separate and BOTH reachable.
   `initial_stream_window_size` and `initial_connection_window_size` are two
   builder calls and they behave as two. Holding the stream window at 4 MiB and
   moving only the connection window: 4 MiB gives 7 stalls, 65,535 gives 4823,
   at t_win=0 with s_win=3655072. Same stream window, two orders of magnitude
   apart in stalls, so the second setting is doing the work -- which is what
   grpc++ has no argument for.

2. DOES AN EXPLICIT WINDOW DISABLE BDP PROBING IN grpc++?
   No, and the interaction runs the other way from grpc-java's.
   With nothing configured the client sends 3 BDP pings and re-announces its
   window 3 time(s), ending at 6799830. Setting the window to 4 MiB and leaving the
   probe alone: 3 pings, 3 announcement(s), ending at 6945113 -- auto-tuning is
   still running, and the configured value is not even what gets announced
   (4194304 asked for, 4194303 announced), because with the estimator on it is a
   starting point the estimator overwrites. Only GRPC_ARG_HTTP2_BDP_PROBE=0
   stops it: 0 pings.
   So on grpc++ the window argument and the probe argument are INDEPENDENT,
   where on grpc-java calling flowControlWindow turns BDP off by itself. An
   arm that set the window and not the probe would be measuring an auto-tuned
   transport while its configuration line claimed a pinned one.

3. AND A THIRD TRAP NEITHER SHAPES.md NOR THIS SLICE HAD: on grpc++, TURNING
   AUTO-TUNING OFF SHRINKS THE WINDOW TO 64 KIB.
   `grpcpp bdp off` -- the probe off and no window set -- announces 65535, not
   the 4194303 the same build announces by default, and it stalls 108 times where
   the default stalls 1. grpc-core's 4 MiB default initial window is the BDP
   estimator's doing; switch the estimator off and the announced window falls
   back to GRPC_ARG_HTTP2_STREAM_LOOKAHEAD_BYTES, whose documented default is
   64 kb. So 'turn auto-tuning off so the arm is deterministic' is, on its own,
   a 64x REDUCTION in the stream window, and a slice that did it without also
   setting the window would have published a flow-control artefact as a
   transport measurement.

4. WHAT EACH STACK'S DEFAULT ACTUALLY IS, and SHAPES.md's table is wrong about
   one of them.
   grpc++ (grpc-core 1.51.1) announces 4194303 with nothing configured -- about 4 MiB,
   not the 65,535 RFC 9113 gives as the protocol's initial value -- and
   auto-tuning is ON.
   tonic 0.14 over hyper 1.11 announces 2097152: hyper's DEFAULT_STREAM_WINDOW, 2
   MiB, with DEFAULT_CONN_WINDOW at 5 MiB and adaptive sizing off
   (hyper/src/proto/h2/client.rs:48-50). **SHAPES.md's table says tonic/hyper is
   at 65,535 with auto-tuning off.** The auto-tuning half is right and the
   window is wrong by a factor of 32.
   The consequence is not cosmetic and it lands on this slice's own published
   log: `logs/cpp/rpc.log` said a 540 KB response 'against a 64 KB default
   stream window means a single call in flight spends most of its wall clock
   waiting for WINDOW_UPDATE'. Neither stack was at 64 KB. 540 KB fits inside
   2 MiB and inside 4 MiB with no stall at all, and the 5314 stalls this probe
   does see are all in configurations that PIN a window, never in a default
   one. That sentence is withdrawn.

5. THE CORE COULD NOT BE PINNED AT ALL until this work unit.
   `ak_client_new` took a URI and nothing else, so cells B and C ran at hyper's
   defaults while cell A ran at grpc-core's, and the two were compared as
   though that were one transport. `ak_client_new_opts` was ADDED to the shared
   core (R0 permits an addition; `ak_client_new` is now a call to it with NULL
   options, which is byte-for-byte the old behaviour). The pinned row is that
   entry point working: 4194304 announced against 2097152 unpinned.

6. WHAT THIS MEANS FOR THE TIMED ARM, and it is not what SHAPES.md assumes.
   SHAPES.md says pin ArmoniK's 4 MiB stream window in every cell and make that
   the headline, with the stack default as a labelled second row. On grpc++ the
   pinned configuration is NOT REACHABLE: the connection window has no
   argument, so pinning the stream window at 4 MiB and the probe off leaves the
   connection window at its un-tuned value and produces 259 stalls that the
   DEFAULT configuration does not have (1). `logs/cpp/rpc.log` therefore
   carries BOTH configurations in full rather than publishing a pinned headline
   whose incumbent is handicapped by the pinning -- which would be an R14
   defect pointed at the incumbent by its own harness.
